= Personas

_Who are you in this chronicle? Six ways to play the same modded world — each with a background, and the game-creation choices that make it yours._

== Before you start: how "creation" works here

Civ 6 has no character creator — your identity comes from the choices in *Advanced Setup* and the rules you keep:

- _The civilization and leader._ This is your kit: unique district, unique units, leader ability. Each persona pairs them with a victory lane.
- _The map and the world._ Map type, size, speed, and game modes shape *how* the story plays out — an archipelago is a different chronicle than a Pangaea.
- _The difficulty and the house rules._ Difficulty sets the opponent's economy; house rules are the real character: what you may do, what you must do, what counts as winning.
- _The mods._ Chapter 2's list is one installation with two layers — the personas are all Layer 2 games, so make sure you have graduated before starting a persona run; each persona leans on the mods that matter to its playstyle and picks its game modes accordingly.
- _The save._ Name each game after its persona (`grand-architect`, `grand-chancellor`, …) and keep one active game per persona — switching personas means starting a new chronicle, not a new install.

#info[
  All six personas run on the same modlist — the personas differ in civ, setup, and self-imposed rules, not in mods. They are _Layer 2 games_: learn the rules on a Layer 1 save (Chapter 1 installs it), then graduate. Keep one save per persona so you can always return to an old chronicle.
]

== 1 · The Architect of Wonders

#persona(
  name: "The Architect of Wonders",
  tag: "Difficulty: King · Focus: culture, wonders, and a skyline that tells your story",
  background: [
    *Background.* You do not conquer the world; you *beautify* it. The Architect measures an empire in wonders per era, themed museums, and cities worth photographing. You pick wonder timings like a craftsman, plan districts like a city planner, and let the culture victory come as the natural by-product of building something worth remembering. Your empire should look as good on the map as it does on the victory screen.
  ],
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Civ & leader:* China led by Qin Shi Huang — the wonder-building, worker-driven kit that rewards exactly your obsession. (Egypt's Cleopatra is the riverside alternative.)
- *Map:* Continents, Standard size, Standard speed, World age New (hills for the views).
- *Game modes:* Monopolies & Corporations on (monopolies multiply tourism); everything else default.
- *House rules:* at least two wonders completed every era; builders must be *earned* — no buying them with gold before the Medieval era; never chop a wonder's production (real architects don't demolish forests for columns).
- *Goal:* three wonders per era, one fully themed museum, and a culture victory — or simply the most beautiful empire at the score screen.
- *Mods that shine:* Detailed Map Tacks (wonder adjacency planning), Extended Policy Cards, Terra Mirabilis (20 new natural wonders to build around), Resourceful 2 (more luxuries to keep your cities happy), Colorized Historic Moments, City Sprawl Graphics, Prettier Lakes.

== 2 · The Iron Chancellor

#persona(
  name: "The Iron Chancellor",
  tag: "Difficulty: King–Emperor · Focus: domination through economy, not zerg",
  background: [
    *Background.* You are a statesman first and a general second: the Iron Chancellor builds an industrial heartland, then wages *surgical* wars — declared with cause, fought with corps, and closed with negotiated peace that extracts a price. You denounce, you demand, you calculate. With Real Strategy installed, the AI fights like a rival great power, which is exactly the opponent you want.
  ],
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Civ & leader:* Germany led by Frederick Barbarossa — extra district per city, the Hansa industrial district, and military city-state affinity.
- *Map:* Pangaea, Standard size, Standard speed (fighting on one continent; an Archipelago run is the veteran's remix).
- *Game modes:* default modes off; Barbarian Clans on (more factions, more politics).
- *House rules:* no war before the Medieval era (chancellors prepare first); every war needs a *casus belli* — denounce first, then demand; never accept peace without a city, a luxury, or a tribute in gold; maintain a standing army of at least six units at all times.
- *Goal:* annex two rivals' capitals, then win by domination — or by score if the world sues for peace first.
- *Mods that shine:* Better Combat Preview (calculate every assault), Extended Diplomacy Ribbon (read the balance of power), Real Strategy (the AI pushes back), Real Tech Tree (plan the military research path), Production Queue (keep the war machine fed).

== 3 · The Cradle of Science

#persona(
  name: "The Cradle of Science",
  tag: "Difficulty: Emperor · Focus: science victory, no shortcuts",
  background: [
    *Background.* You are a research institute with a flag. Every campus is a cathedral, every eureka a doctrine, and the space race is the only race you recognize. You out-tech your neighbors until the game is decided in your favor — not because the game was easy, but because you read it better. The Cradle plays clean: no gold-purchased progress, no exploited cheese — just the long compounding arc of a knowledge economy.
  ],
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Civ & leader:* Korea led by Seondeok — a campus-adjacency kit built for exactly this lane. (Scotland is the alternative for the happiness/tech angle.)
- *Map:* Continents, Standard size, Standard speed.
- *Game modes:* default modes off — a clean ruleset is the point.
- *House rules:* every city must contain a campus (settle accordingly); gold may *never* buy buildings or projects — everything must be produced or built; no reloading, ever; at least one research alliance maintained from the Medieval era on.
- *Goal:* science victory; the personal target is being first to the Information era tech tree's crown.
- *Mods that shine:* Detailed Map Tacks (campus adjacency), Better Report Screen (the empire-wide dashboard), Better Tech Tree + Real Tech Tree (chart the historical research path), Real Eurekas (adapt to each game's boosts), Production Queue (space projects without idle cities).

== 4 · The Merchant Republic

#persona(
  name: "The Merchant Republic",
  tag: "Difficulty: King · Focus: trade, gold, and winning the world congress",
  background: [
    *Background.* You were born with a ledger in hand. The Merchant Republic wins by being *indispensable*: every neighbor trades with you, every city-state Suzerain owes you a favour, and the world congress eventually votes for the republic that bankrolls the world. You measure your empire in trade routes at capacity, gold in the treasury, and diplomatic favour in the bank. When war comes — and it will — you buy the peace.
  ],
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Civ & leader:* Portugal led by João III — the trade-route economy, built for foreign ports and gold. (Netherlands is the riverside alternative.)
- *Map:* Archipelago, Standard size, Standard speed — a maritime world where routes and ports matter.
- *Game modes:* Monopolies & Corporations _on_ (the mode this persona is built for); Secret Societies optional if you want a splash of fantasy.
- *House rules:* trade routes must run at capacity from the moment they unlock; gold must be reinvested within five turns (build, buy, or gift — no hoarding); every city-state you meet gets at least one envoy.
- *Goal:* diplomatic victory — or 100,000 gold in the treasury, whichever comes first.
- *Mods that shine:* Quick Deals (the market at a keystroke), Better Trade Screen (route yield sorting), Global Relations Panel (who owes whom), Resourceful 2 + Luxury Resource Variety (the monopoly raw material), Sukritact's Resources.

== 5 · The Faithful Pilgrim

#persona(
  name: "The Faithful Pilgrim",
  tag: "Difficulty: King · Focus: religion, faith economy, and the slow conversion",
  background: [
    *Background.* You do not rush. The Pilgrim's faith spreads like a tide: founded early, defended patiently, and carried on trade routes and missionary trails to every corner of the map. Yours is the quiet victory — no armies, no rockets, just the quiet compounding of belief until the world converts around you. The modded game rewards this patience: a faith economy funds districts, buildings, and even the late-game culture push.
  ],
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Civ & leader:* Russia led by Peter the Great — the faith-and-culture-from-tundra kit with the Lavra district. (Byzantium is the aggressive alternative.)
- *Map:* Continents, Standard size, Standard speed.
- *Game modes:* default modes off.
- *House rules:* found a religion by turn 100 or restart the chronicle; every Holy Site must be *built*, never bought; convert at least one city in every other civilization — including rivals you never meet peacefully (trade routes and apostles do the job).
- *Goal:* religious victory, converted cleanly — and a faith stockpile to fund the endgame as a bonus.
- *Mods that shine:* More Lenses (holy-site adjacency), Extended Policy Cards (faith policies), Real Great People (the overview that plans your prophet and apostles), Better Report Screen.

== 6 · The Chronicler of Eras

#persona(
  name: "The Chronicler of Eras",
  tag: "Difficulty: Prince · Focus: the living world — no victory hunting",
  background: [
    *Background.* You play the long game that isn't a game: the Chronicler builds a *world* and lets history happen to it. Wars, golden ages, floods, and revolutions all belong in the record. You name your cities in the tongue of the era (Rosetta does the renaming), pause at era transitions to admire the map, and let Dramatic Ages toss your empire up and down. Victory is optional — the chronicle is the point. Screenshots welcome.
  ],
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Civ & leader:* your pick — Rome led by Trajan is the classic empire-building arc; Japan or Egypt change the flavor of the chronicle.
- *Map:* YnAMP _True Start Earth_ (or Continents), up to Enormous size, _Epic_ speed — the world gets time to breathe.
- *Game modes:* Barbarian Clans and Dramatic Ages _on_ (history should bite); Sukritact's Oceans on if you use a standard map script instead of YnAMP.
- *House rules:* never chase a victory type — pursue whatever the era demands (era score decides); never restart a save, never reload a disaster; screenshot every era transition; write the story in your head as you go.
- *Goal:* a world history you would retell — and the highest era score you can honestly earn.
- *Mods that shine:* the whole World & Content stack — YnAMP, Rosetta, Terra Mirabilis, Wetlands, Sukritact's Resources (+ Oceans), Resourceful 2, Environment Skin, Denser Vegetation, City Sprawl Graphics, Colorized Historic Moments, Hillier Hills, Prettier Lakes — plus Real Tech Tree and, if you want the eras to breathe, Extended Eras.

== How to start a persona run

1. Create a game in *Advanced Setup* with the persona's settings from above.
2. Name the save `grand-<persona>` so the chronicles stay separate.
3. Write your house rules on paper — three lines of rules beat a forgotten promise in turn 200.
4. Enable the persona's game modes and play the first era following its priorities.

